    89	// the pending list. Two kinds of admitted section, each with an in-flight count and a per-thread depth:
    90	//  * kRelease: one producer deleter call (ManagedOwner::reset, so every release path: the immediate release of a
    91	//    refused call while unwinding, the next-call drain, synchronize(), the bound drain, the fallback sync of
    92	//    ~RetainUntilComplete) and one drop of the last reference to an unconsumed DLPack capsule (CapsuleRef::drop: the
    93	//    producer's capsule destructor calls its deleter);
    94	//  * kCallback: the producer's Python callbacks in to_capsule (attribute lookups, __dlpack_device__, __dlpack__).
    95	// Protocol:
    96	//  * admit(k): if the interpreter is not accepting Python (not initialized, or finalizing: second line of defence, as
    97	//    core's interpreter_accepts_gil_state), refuse; else increment in_flight[k], THEN read `closing`; if it is set, back
    98	//    out (decrement, notify) and refuse; else ++t_depth[k] and run the section. leave(k): --t_depth[k], decrement,
    99	//    notify. `closing` and the counts are sequentially consistent atomics: admit is "increment, then read closing", the
   100	//    exit hook is "set closing, then read the counts", so at least one side sees the other (the store-buffering
   101	//    handshake: either the releaser sees closing and backs out, or the hook sees the count and waits).
   102	//  * A refused release is never run: the deleter is not called (the manager is leaked, counted), the capsule reference
   103	//    is kept (counted); no producer code and no Python is entered. A refused producer callback raises RuntimeError
   104	//    before calling it (nothing acquired).
   105	//  * close_admission_and_wait (tc_signal's atexit hook, GIL held): set `closing` for good, then wait until no admitted
   106	//    section of ANOTHER thread is in flight (in_flight[k] <= this thread's t_depth[k] for both kinds: self-exclusion, so
   107	//    a hook running inside its own callback does not wait for itself). CPython 3.12.3 runs atexit callbacks before it
   108	//    marks the runtime finalizing (Py_FinalizeEx: _PyAtExit_Call, then _PyInterpreterState_SetFinalizing /
   109	//    _PyRuntimeState_SetFinalizing; verified by P1T-FIX2 in pylifecycle.c lines 1815 and 1832-1833), so once the hook
   110	//    returns no admitted add-on callback is running, and every later one is refused: nothing in the add-on can take the
   111	//    GIL after finalization begins.
   112	//  * The wait has NO time bound (round-3 V3-01: a bounded return lets finalization overtake the callback). After a delay
   113	//    (tc_signal._RELEASE_EXIT_DIAG_S, default 5 s) it writes one stderr line, then another each time the time waited
   114	//    has doubled. Consequence: a producer deleter, capsule destructor or __dlpack__ that never returns makes interpreter
   115	//    exit wait for it (the producer's contract violation; waiting is the honest outcome, an early return aborts).
   116	//  * GIL: the waiting thread releases the GIL with PyEval_SaveThread (an admitted callback that needs the GIL can then
   117	//    finish), waits on the condition variable with no Python object touched, drops the mutex, and only then re-takes
   118	//    the GIL with PyEval_RestoreThread, called explicitly in a NON-noexcept frame (not from a destructor such as
   119	//    py::gil_scoped_release's): if CPython exits a waiting daemon thread there during finalization, the forced unwind
   120	//    travels through ordinary frames to pybind11's dispatcher, which rethrows abi::__forced_unwind (pybind11 v2.12
   121	//    pybind11.h:1037), instead of meeting a noexcept boundary. No thread waits for the GIL while holding the mutex.
