   535	// P1T-FIX2, review finding N4: a producer deleter that throws a C++ exception must not terminate the process. Every
   536	// deleter call in tc_signal goes through reset() (the immediate release of a refused call, the deferred drain, the
   537	// bound drain, the fallback sync, synchronize() and the atexit hook all end here), and reset() is noexcept, so an
   538	// escaping exception used to reach std::terminate (round-2 probe cpu_native throw_deleter, rc -6). Now each call is
   539	// wrapped: the exception is caught (catch-all), counted (pending_stats()['deleter_exceptions']) and dropped; the owner
   540	// pointer is cleared BEFORE the call, so a deleter is never called twice, and the caller (a loop over an entry's
   541	// managers) keeps going with the remaining managers. The DLPack ABI declares the deleter as a C function, so a
   542	// throwing deleter already breaks the producer's contract; containing it is the consumer's choice, not a recovery
   543	// (the producer's memory may or may not have been freed: tc_signal does not touch it again either way).
   544	// Prior art: C++11 noexcept semantics ([except.terminate]: an exception escaping a noexcept function calls
   545	// std::terminate) and the "destructors/cleanup must not throw" guideline (C++ Core Guidelines C.36/E.16, Sutter &
   546	// Alexandrescu 2004, item 51). Taken: catch at the noexcept boundary. Ours: counting and continuing per manager.
   547	// Known gap (not closed): a deleter that calls pthread_exit or is cancelled unwinds with abi::__forced_unwind, which
   548	// may not be swallowed (glibc aborts); no C++ handler can contain that.
   549	// P1T-FIX5 (V5-01): the forced unwind that round 5 produced was not the producer's: CPython exited the thread when the
   550	// deleter re-took the GIL during finalization. Every deleter call is now an admitted release (release lifecycle above):
   551	// the exit hook does not return while one is running, and once it has closed admission no deleter is called at all, so
   552	// CPython never finalizes under a running deleter and no forced unwind of that origin can reach this catch-all. What
   553	// this catch-all does with a forced unwind is unchanged: it swallows it, and glibc then aborts ("FATAL: exception not
   554	// rethrown"); rethrowing would instead meet the noexcept of reset() (std::terminate). Either way that remains the
   555	// producer's own pthread_exit / cancellation, a DLPack contract violation (the deleter is a C function that returns).
   556	namespace {
   557	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_deleter_exceptions{0};  // static storage: counting never allocates
   558	
   559	template <typename M>
   560	void call_deleter_contained(M* m) noexcept {
   561	  if (m == nullptr || m->deleter == nullptr) return;
   562	  // P1T-FIX5 (V5-01): admitted release, or deliberately leaked (deleter never called, counted) after admission closed.
   563	  const Admission a = admit(kRelease);
   564	  if (a != Admission::kAdmitted) {
   565	    (a == Admission::kClosed ? g_deleters_leaked_after_close : g_deleters_leaked_finalizing)
   566	        .fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
   567	    return;
   568	  }
   569	  try {
   570	    m->deleter(m);
   571	  } catch (...) {
   572	    g_deleter_exceptions.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
   573	  }
   574	  leave(kRelease);  // reached on both outcomes: an ordinary C++ exception is contained, and the section still leaves
   575	}
   576	}  // namespace
   577	
   578	void ManagedOwner::reset() noexcept {
   579	  // DLPack consumer rule: when done, call the producer's deleter (it may be NULL). Called with the GIL held (every
   580	  // tc_signal entry point runs under the GIL), so a deleter that touches Python objects is safe. P1T-FIX5: each call
   581	  // is an admitted release (call_deleter_contained).
   582	  if (legacy != nullptr) {
   583	    DLManagedTensor* m = legacy;
   584	    legacy = nullptr;  // cleared first: never called twice, even if the deleter throws or re-enters
   585	    call_deleter_contained(m);
   586	  }
   587	  if (versioned != nullptr) {
   588	    DLManagedTensorVersioned* m = versioned;
   589	    versioned = nullptr;
   590	    call_deleter_contained(m);
   591	  }
   592	}
   593	
   594	unsigned long long deleter_exceptions() noexcept { return g_deleter_exceptions.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); }
