   714	// ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ deferred release (F01)
   715	// Design and prior art: dlpack_operand.h, RetainUntilComplete. Every function here runs on the calling host thread with
   716	// the GIL held (every _tc_signal entry point is a pybind11 function that keeps the GIL), so the pending list needs no
   717	// lock. Deleters may run Python (a ctypes producer) and may even re-enter tc_signal; therefore an entry is always taken
   718	// OFF the list before its deleters run, and nothing holds a reference into the list across a deleter call.
   719	//
   720	// P1T-FIX2 (round-2 review N1, N3) changed the release paths as follows:
   721	//  * N3, no allocation failure terminates. The noexcept functions here (drain_completed, the RetainUntilComplete
   722	//    destructor, sync_and_release_all_impl, recycle_event) no longer allocate, except the pending-list push in the
   723	//    destructor, which is caught and falls back to a synchronous release (counted). sync_and_release_all_impl works on
   724	//    the live list in place (phase 1 synchronizes the devices into a fixed-capacity set, phase 2 pops completed
   725	//    entries), instead of a take-all std::deque swap and a std::set / std::vector of devices, which allocated inside a
   726	//    noexcept function (libstdc++'s deque allocates even when default-constructed: the round-2 probe oom_sync, rc -6).
   727	//    The recycle slot of every completion event is reserved when the event is created (constructor path, before any
   728	//    launch), so recycling never allocates. The owner vector is reserved in the constructor, as before.
   729	//  * N1, no CUDA error is reported as success: a failed current-device query (cudaGetDevice) is now an error of
   730	//    synchronize() (it used to be skipped, so an empty list "synchronized" successfully on a machine with no usable
   731	//    device: probe empty_sync); a stream sync that fails is an error; and a release-path failure that happens where
   732	//    nothing can raise (the fallback sync and the bound drain inside the destructor) is recorded and raised by the next
   733	//    synchronize() (once), and logged by the atexit hook.
   734	// Prior art for the in-place two-phase drain: PyTorch's CUDA caching allocator processes its event list in place from
   735	// the host (process_events: query each recorded event, stop at the first one not ready). Taken: query-and-pop in FIFO
   736	// order. Ours: the sync-then-pop split with the per-device failure set, and leak-instead-of-release for entries whose
   737	// device could not be synchronized. Fixed-capacity, allocation-free bookkeeping on failure paths is ordinary
   738	// "no allocation on the error path" practice (e.g. the C++ Core Guidelines E.16 discussion); no specific prior art
   739	// claimed for the set itself.
   740	namespace test_hooks {
   741	namespace {
   742	std::atomic<unsigned> g_armed{0};
   743	}  // namespace
   744	void arm(unsigned bits) noexcept { g_armed.fetch_or(bits, std::memory_order_relaxed); }
   745	void clear() noexcept { g_armed.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed); }
   746	unsigned armed() noexcept { return g_armed.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); }
   747	bool take(unsigned bit) noexcept {
   748	  if ((g_armed.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) & bit) == 0) return false;  // the production path: one relaxed load
   749	  return (g_armed.fetch_and(~bit, std::memory_order_relaxed) & bit) != 0;
   750	}
   751	}  // namespace test_hooks
