  1394	// P1T-FIX7, round-7 review finding V7-01 (MAJOR): the exit close-and-wait is reached on every path, whatever the
  1395	// diagnostic knob holds. Native side of the fix (the Python side is tc_signal/__init__.py _release_pending_at_exit):
  1396	//  * the close and the wait take a delay that is diagnostics only; the raw entry (bindings.cpp raw_exit_close_admission)
  1397	//    reads the knob at C level and, for anything it cannot read (another type, an int beyond the double range, the
  1398	//    knob deleted), uses tuning::kExitDiagDefaultS and STILL closes and waits (exit_close_admission below);
  1399	//  * the atexit runner (bindings.cpp raw_exit_hook_runner) calls the Python hook and, if the hook raised (for example
  1400	//    an asynchronous KeyboardInterrupt delivered at its first bytecode, before any statement of it ran), closes and
  1401	//    waits itself with the default delay (exit_runner_close_and_wait), then logs the failures nobody reported
  1402	//    (exit_log_unreported). It does nothing when the hook returned, so a hook that returns without closing is a
  1403	//    hook defect the tests and the round-7 probes see, not one the runner hides.
  1404	// The counts of the two exit paths are kept apart (exit_hook_calls / exit_runner_closes), so a test can tell which
  1405	// one waited. Prior art: CPython's own atexit, which runs each callback, prints (does not propagate) an exception
  1406	// escaping it and still runs the remaining callbacks (taken: an exception in one exit step does not cancel the next);
  1407	// Python's try / finally guarantee, which also holds for KeyboardInterrupt (language reference, "The try statement")
  1408	// but cannot cover a frame's first bytecode, hence the native runner; CPython's threading._shutdown (an unconditional
  1409	// exit-time join). Ours: making the close independent of the knob's readability and of exceptions raised before it.
  1410	namespace {
  1411	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_exit_runner_closes{0};
  1412	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_exit_runner_in_flight_at_close{0};
  1413	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_exit_diag_default_used{0};
  1414	
  1415	struct ExitClose {
  1416	  WaitOutcome w;
  1417	  long long pending_at_close = 0;
  1418	};
  1419	
  1420	// Close admission for good, then wait for every admitted section of another thread. No Python object is touched (the
  1421	// GIL is released for the wait inside wait_for_other_threads). Never returns before the wait has finished.
  1422	ExitClose exit_close_and_wait(double first_diag_s) {
  1423	  Lifecycle& lc = lifecycle();
  1424	  lc.closing.store(true, std::memory_order_seq_cst);  // admission closed for good: nothing new is admitted
  1425	  ExitClose c;
  1426	  c.pending_at_close = static_cast<long long>(pending().q.size());  // never released from here on
  1427	  c.w = wait_for_other_threads(first_diag_s);
  1428	  return c;
  1429	}
  1430	}  // namespace
