  1510	  // P1T-FIX4 (sweep, CUDA error state): every launch of every entry point follows this constructor, and each launch is
  1511	  // checked by reading the calling thread's last-error slot. 62c8d9f never looked at the slot BEFORE the first launch,
  1512	  // so an error already pending there (a refused launch elsewhere in this thread, a failure some other code did not
  1513	  // consume) was reported after this call's launches as this call's own launch failure, with the work queued anyway.
  1514	  // Now it is taken here, right before the first launch, and raised as what it is: RuntimeError naming the pending
  1515	  // error, nothing launched, and the operands released at once by their own destructors (nothing was queued on them;
  1516	  // the completion event goes back to its pool). From here on a launch check can only report this call's own error.
  1517	  // Placed after acquire_event so a machine with no usable device keeps its earlier refusal ("cannot create the
  1518	  // completion event", cudaErrorNoDevice, which the runtime keeps in the slot permanently: receipt
  1519	  // artifacts/p1t_fix4/cuda_semantics.txt).
  1520	  // Prior art: CUDA 12.6 Programming Guide 3.2.12: "one has to make sure that the runtime error variable is set to
  1521	  // cudaSuccess just before the kernel launch, for example, by calling cudaGetLastError() just before the kernel
  1522	  // launch" (artifacts/p1t_fix4/cuda_doc_quotes.txt). Taken: that rule. Ours: raising the pending error as pre-existing
  1523	  // instead of discarding it, at the one constructor every launch path shares.
