  1252	  // Device::from_string (pre-existing core code) labels every CUDA string index 0, so with another device current
  1253	  // 62c8d9f returned memory of that device labeled cuda:0 (and cuda:N was silently ignored). Now a CUDA tensor is
  1254	  // labeled with the current device, read with a checked cudaGetDevice (RuntimeError naming it, slot entry consumed),
  1255	  // and an explicit "cuda:N" must name the current device (ValueError otherwise; TensorCUDA does not switch devices to
  1256	  // allocate). The label then equals the allocation's device, which the P1-T export and fill_ prove before use.
  1257	  // Prior art: torch.empty(device=...) / cupy.empty under cupy.cuda.Device (an allocation belongs to an explicit,
  1258	  // recorded device). Taken: label = allocation device. Ours: refusing an index other than the current device.
